<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pepin of Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">777–<a href="/years/810">810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Pippin of Italy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pepin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, born Carloman, who was crowned king of Italy in 781 to rule the conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdom. He campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. In 810 he attacked the Venetian lagoon, and his failure to take the islands helped move the seat of Venetian government to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a>. He died later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy.jpg" alt="Pepin of Italy" width="279" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9pin_d%27Italie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pepin was born Carloman in 777, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and his wife Hildegard, and had an older brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-younger">Charles the Younger</a>, and a half-brother, Pepin the Hunchback. In 781 Charlemagne and Hildegard took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-i">Pope Adrian I</a>, with his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> and his sisters Rotrude and Bertha. His parents had delayed his baptism so that the pope could perform it. Adrian baptised him, renamed him Pepin, and crowned him king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, later styled king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, while Louis was crowned king of Aquitaine. The name was probably chosen to recall his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, remembered as a firm ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Although only four years old, Pepin was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> to live under advisers provided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, the most important of whom were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalard-of-corbie">Adalard of Corbie</a>, Waldo of Reichenau, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duke Rotchild and Angilbert. His court was based chiefly at Verona, with palaces also at Mantua and at Pavia, the traditional Lombard capital. He was king in his own name, but Charlemagne took a strong hand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> into Pepin's adulthood and sometimes issued laws directly.</p><p>Pepin took part in his father's campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III of Bavaria</a> in 786. In 796 he led a campaign against the Avar Khaganate, took its stronghold and precipitated the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> state, which allowed the Frankish realm to expand eastward; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem De Pippini regis Victoria Avarica commemorates the victory. He also led several raids against the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and, in 810, a campaign against the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In 806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s Divisio Regnorum confirmed Pepin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and gave him most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Alamannia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pepin died on 8 July 810. Over 810 and 811 his sister Rotrude, his aunt Gisela, his half-brother Pepin and his brother Charles also died, possibly victims of an epidemic that had spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> then declared Pepin's son Bernard ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Louis the heir to the rest of the empire. Pepin had married Theodrada, a cousin of his father, and Louis later used the closeness of the relation to portray the marriage as illegitimate in order to sideline Bernard. Bernard's male-line descendants ruled as counts of Vermandois into the eleventh century, the longest-surviving direct male line of Charlemagne. Pepin also had five daughters and was an ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pepin of Italy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the same year Duke Eric of Friuli sent a force that plundered the Ring, and in 796 Charlemagne's son <b>Pippin of Italy</b> destroyed it and carried its treasure to Aachen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two of Charlemagne's three legitimate adult sons, Charles the Younger and <b>Pepin of Italy</b>, died before him, which allowed Louis the Pious to inherit the undivided empire in 814 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 810 <b>Pepin of Italy</b>, son of Charlemagne, attacked the lagoon and overran Chioggia and Pellestrina but failed to subdue the central islands, and the Peace of Aachen of 812 left Venice under nominal Byzantine sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 781 Pope Hadrian I crowned his young son <b>Pepin</b> King of Italy, creating a subordinate kingdom with its capital at Pavia, covering northern and central Italy apart from Venice and the papal lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/carolingian-kingdom-of-italy">Carolingian Kingdom of Italy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=798" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Pepin of Italy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=798">What was here in 798 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
